    "topic_code": "2.12",
    "topic_title": "Sampling Distributions and the Central Limit Theorem",
    "stem": "The commute times of all workers in a large city form a strongly right-skewed distribution. A researcher takes 500 separate random samples of 50 workers each, computes the mean commute time of each sample, and makes a dotplot of those 500 sample means. Which statement best describes this dotplot?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "Each dot is one worker's commute time, so the dotplot shows the population distribution and should be right-skewed.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This misreads what a dot represents, treating a plot of summary values as a plot of individuals. The researcher plotted 500 sample means, not individual commute times, so this is not the population distribution. Fix: Before describing any distribution, ask whether one dot stands for one individual or for one whole sample's statistic."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "Because many samples were taken, the Central Limit Theorem makes the population of commute times approximately normal, so the dotplot shows a normal population.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This is the classic error of attaching the Central Limit Theorem to the population. Sampling never changes the population; individual commute times stay right-skewed, and only the distribution of the sample mean becomes approximately normal. Fix: When you invoke the Central Limit Theorem, check that your normality claim refers to the distribution of sample means, not the population."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "Each dot is one sample's mean, so the dotplot approximates the sampling distribution of the sample mean, which should be approximately normal even though the population is skewed.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Each dot summarizes a whole sample of 50 workers, so the plot shows how a statistic varies across samples. The Central Limit Theorem says the sampling distribution of a sample mean is approximately normal for a large enough sample size. This holds even though the individual commute times remain right-skewed."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "Because the dotplot was built by repeating a random process many times, it is a randomization distribution showing outcomes when the data are reshuffled among groups.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This confuses repeated sampling with reallocation. A randomization distribution comes from reshuffling one set of data among groups, but here new random samples were drawn from the population each time, which approximates a sampling distribution. Fix: Ask whether each repetition draws a new sample from the population or reshuffles existing data among groups before naming the distribution."
     }
    ]
   },
